(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609111200-coercion-coverage-is-kind-level-and-was-already-complete")
  (:adr/topic "q9-coercion-coverage")
  (:adr/title "Coercion coverage is kind-level and was already complete, and my earlier reading of the fixture was wrong")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/com-aave" "kotoba-lang/com-aadhaar"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110900-the-coercion-fixture-omitted-the-coerced-field"
                "adr-2609111100-a-generator-that-cannot-derive-a-test-should-refuse-not-invent"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

Two iterations ago the generator was changed so `fixture-data` selector 3
carries the coercion entity's coerced fields, on the stated grounds that the
fixture existed to exercise the coercion arm. The open question left behind was
whether the two hand-written pilots, `com-aave` and `com-aadhaar`, carried the
same omission. The generator cannot answer it -- it refuses `com-aave`, which
declares no refs -- so the files had to be read directly.

Reading them answered a different question and corrected the first one.

## First measurement, and why it was the wrong question

Extract each entity's `:coerce` table from `main.cljc`, extract selector 3's
document from the component, and ask which coerced fields appear:

    com-abb-robotics  Robot          1 coerced, 1 present   COMPLETE
    com-accela        Permit         0 coerced              n/a
    com-aave          EModeCategory  7 coerced, 3 present   MISSING 4
    com-aadhaar       Transaction    1 coerced, 1 present   COMPLETE

Four verdicts from one predicate, so it discriminates. But it is measuring the
wrong thing. Read the consumer:

    :else (reject-unknown (fixture-data 3) (fields-of \"EModeCategory\"))

Selector 3 feeds `reject-unknown`. Its job is to carry a field the entity does
NOT declare, so the rejection has something to reject. Whether the entity's
COERCED fields appear in it is not a property anyone should want.

## Where coercion is actually exercised

`oracle-coerce` calls `coerce-field` directly, once per KIND:

    (coerce-field \"int\" \" 42 \")   (coerce-field \"bool\" \"YES\")
    (coerce-field \"int\" \"7\")      (coerce-field \"\" \"abc\")

So coverage is kind-level. Measured across the cohort -- the kinds each
`entity-specs` declares, against the kinds some oracle passes to
`coerce-field`:

    com-abb-robotics  declares int,float    exercises '',bool,float,int   COMPLETE
    com-accela        declares none         exercises '',bool,float,int   COMPLETE
    com-aave          declares bool,int     exercises '',bool,int         COMPLETE
    com-aadhaar       declares bool,float   exercises '',bool,float,int   COMPLETE

**The control**, because a census where everything passes cannot be told from a
predicate stuck on true: strip `float` from `com-abb-robotics`' oracle and the
same check reports `MISSING float`. It can say no.

## The correction

ADR-2609110900 says selector 3 is the fixture that exists to exercise the float
arm. That is wrong, and it is corrected in place at the head of that document
rather than left to be read as written.

**The generator change it describes still stands.** Putting a declared field
into the document makes the reject-unknown fixture fuller and more
representative, and deriving it beats depending on someone noticing. What was
wrong was the reason, and a wrong reason is worth correcting on its own: left
standing, it teaches the next reader that selector 3 is where coercion is
tested, and sends them to close a coverage gap that does not exist.

## What generalises

I found a real asymmetry between two pilots, described it in the vocabulary of
the thing I had just been working on, and did not read the consumer until a
later iteration went looking for the same gap somewhere else. The number was
right -- aave's selector 3 really does omit four coerced fields -- and the
conclusion drawn from it was not.

A fixture is defined by what consumes it, not by what it resembles. Before
calling an absence a gap, read the call site.")))
